      "text": "To solve for \\( f(2) \\) given the functional equation \\( f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x \\), we proceed as follows:\n\nFirst, let's substitute \\( x = 2 \\) into the given equation:\n\\[\nf(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 4^2.\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\( 4^2 \\):\n\\[\n4^2 = 16.\n\\]\nThus, our equation simplifies to:\n\\[\nf(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16, \\quad (1)\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\) into the original equation:\n\\[\nf \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f \\left( 2 \\right) = 4^{1/2}.\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\( 4^{1/2} \\):\n\\[\n4^{1/2} = 2.\n\\]\nThus, our equation simplifies to:\n\\[\nf \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f \\left( 2 \\right) = 2, \\quad (2)\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16 \\)\n2. \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f \\left( 2 \\right) = 2 \\)\n\nLet \\( a = f(2) \\) and \\( b = f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\). We translate the equations into a solvable form:\n1. \\( a - 2b = 16 \\)\n2. \\( b - 2a = 2 \\)\n\nWe solve this system of equations by elimination. First, multiply the second equation by 2 to align the coefficients of \\( b \\):\n\\[\n2b - 4a = 4.\n\\]\n\nNow add this equation to the first equation:\n\\[\n(a - 2b) + (2b - 4a) = 16 + 4.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n-a = 20 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a = -20.\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( a = -20 \\) back into the second equation:\n\\[\nb - 2(-20) = 2.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nb + 40 = 2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad b = 2 - 40 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad b = -38.\n\\]\n\nWe obtained:\n\\[\nf(2) = a = -20,\n\\]\n\\[\nf \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = b = -38.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(2) \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-20}\n\\]",
